package teyru

// Networking: TCP sockets, shaped like java.net.
//
// The C half lives in internal/runtime/src/tyrt_net.c, which the compiler
// copies into every build. What that file cannot do is throw: a Teyru exception
// declared in the prelude has no tyclass the runtime can name -- the compiler
// installs a fixed list of built-ins into runtime globals at startup, and a
// class declared in lib/ is not on it -- so every helper there answers with an
// integer instead. The convention is documented in that file and in
// internal/runtime/src/tyrt_net.h: a negative result is -errno, a non-negative
// one is a count, a descriptor or a port, a read that returns 0 has met the end
// of the stream and a read that returns -EAGAIN has run out of time. Everything
// in this file exists to turn those integers back into exceptions, so that no
// caller of Socket ever sees one.
//
// Every native method is a `public static native` in `Net`, which is final.
// That is not tidiness: the compiler replaces a native call with a direct call
// to its C helper only when the method is static, private, final, or owned by a
// class with no subclasses, and the generated program repeats the helper's
// prototype at each call site (see the `proto` field in
// internal/codegen/native.go). A class that could be subclassed takes the other
// path -- a stub whose body does not carry the prototype -- and the C would no
// longer compile. Keeping every native static and in one final class means the
// constraint can never be broken by subclassing Socket, which is a thing a
// program may well want to do.
//
// Descriptors are plain ints held in a field, and the C takes them as an
// argument. Nothing in C reads a generated struct, so a field added to Socket
// cannot silently change what the runtime finds at an offset -- the trap the
// PrintStream.target field documents.
//
// TLS is here as well, and it is a socket rather than a second protocol stack:
// the handshake happens on a connection that was made the plaintext way and
// from then on the bytes go through it (TlsSocket, TlsServer and Tls at the end
// of this file). What that costs is worth saying where it can be read: the TLS
// layer is the one part of the runtime written against a library the compiler
// does not ship, so a build compiles it, and links OpenSSL for it, only when
// the program can reach one of its natives -- and a target that has no OpenSSL
// is refused by name rather than left to the linker.
//
// What this is not: there are no threads in the language, so there is no
// thread-per-connection server here and no pool. Two servers in one process do
// work, because a socket is a descriptor and nothing about it is global, but a
// program that accepts on two at once has to take turns. A timed read or accept
// (setSoTimeout) is what stops that from being a dead end: it is the only way
// to wait on more than one thing at a time in a program with one thread.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- exceptions
//
// Java's hierarchy, cut down to the part a socket and a file can produce. They
// extend Exception rather than RuntimeException, as java.io does: a program
// that talks to the network or the disk is expected to say what it does when
// the other end is not there.

class IOException extends Exception {
  public IOException() {
  }
  public IOException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
  public IOException(String message, Throwable cause) {
    super(message, cause)
  }
}

// A socket that failed for a reason that is not specific to connecting,
// resolving or timing out: a refused connection, a broken pipe, a write to a
// socket the peer has closed.
class SocketException extends IOException {
  public SocketException() {
  }
  public SocketException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// The one that must never be confused with the end of a stream. A server that
// reads a request under a timeout needs to tell "the client is slow" apart from
// "the client is gone", because the first is worth waiting for and the second
// is a connection to close.
class SocketTimeoutException extends IOException {
  public SocketTimeoutException() {
  }
  public SocketTimeoutException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

class UnknownHostException extends IOException {
  public UnknownHostException() {
  }
  public UnknownHostException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// Raised by readFully and readLine's callers when the stream ended in the
// middle of what was being read. read() and read(byte[],int,int) report the
// same event as -1, which is Java's contract and what a loop checks for.
class EOFException extends IOException {
  public EOFException() {
  }
  public EOFException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// Raised by readLine(max) when a line does not end within the limit it was
// given. How long a line may be is the protocol's business -- a request line, a
// header, a chunk size line -- so this reader says only that the line it was
// asked for is longer than the caller said it could be, and the caller turns
// that into whatever its protocol answers.
class LineTooLongException extends IOException {
  public LineTooLongException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// The TLS layer's failures, cut the same way the socket's are: one base for
// everything that can go wrong inside a TLS connection, and a class per thing
// that a caller may reasonably want to tell apart. java.net.ssl has the same
// split (SSLException, SSLHandshakeException, SSLPeerUnverifiedException) under
// different names, and the names here say what happened rather than which part
// of the protocol noticed.
class TlsException extends IOException {
  public TlsException() {
  }
  public TlsException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// The certificate the server sent is not trusted. It is the failure a client
// that verifies gets from a self-signed certificate, an expired one, or a chain
// that does not reach a trust anchor, and its message carries the reason
// OpenSSL gave, because "the certificate is not trusted" is only half an answer.
class TlsCertificateException extends TlsException {
  public TlsCertificateException() {
  }
  public TlsCertificateException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// The certificate is trusted but not for this name: the chain verified against
// the anchors the client was given and the host in the URL is not one the
// certificate covers. It is a separate class from the one above because it is a
// separate problem with a separate fix -- the wrong URL, or a server with the
// wrong certificate -- and because a client that reported it as "not trusted"
// would send its user looking at the trust store.
class TlsHostnameException extends TlsException {
  public TlsHostnameException() {
  }
  public TlsHostnameException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// The handshake failed for something that is neither: no shared protocol
// version or cipher, a peer that speaks another protocol entirely, a connection
// that went away in the middle, or a record that did not authenticate.
class TlsHandshakeException extends TlsException {
  public TlsHandshakeException() {
  }
  public TlsHandshakeException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// The certificate or the key cannot be used at all: the file is not a PEM
// document, or the key does not belong to the certificate. A server that cannot
// present its certificate cannot serve, so this is raised where the pair is
// given to it rather than on the first connection.
class TlsConfigException extends TlsException {
  public TlsConfigException() {
  }
  public TlsConfigException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the C surface
//
// Every `0`-suffixed method below is implemented in C, in
// internal/runtime/src/tyrt_net.c, and internal/codegen/native_net.go maps the
// signature onto the helper. Nothing outside this class calls one, so the
// status codes never leave this file.
//
// The body each one carries does not run. It is there because `native` cannot
// be used here, and the reason is worth writing down. The compiler implements a
// prelude class's native method by generating a body that calls the helper
// directly -- without the prototype the call sites carry (see emitSynthetic in
// internal/codegen/emit_synth.go, which reads nativeFn.fn and ignores
// nativeFn.proto). That is fine for every prelude class whose helper tyrt.h
// declares, and impossible for this layer: tyrt.h is not a file this feature
// may edit, and the runtime's files are copied into a build without a header of
// their own, so a declaration of these helpers exists nowhere the generated
// program can see it.
// What the layer needs is the prototype repeated at each call site, which is
// what nativeFn.proto is for -- and the call site takes that path only when the
// method is not `native` and so has no generated body.
//
// So the body is the price of shipping the C in a file of its own. It throws
// rather than returning a plausible number: if a call ever reaches it, the
// compiler has stopped substituting, and a program that says so is worth more
// than one that quietly binds port 0.
final class Net {
  // A descriptor, or -errno. TY_NET_TIMEOUT (-EAGAIN) when the wait expired,
  // TY_NET_UNKNOWN_HOST when the name did not resolve.
  public static int listen0(int port, int backlog, boolean reuse) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.listen0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int connect0(String host, int port, int timeoutMs) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.connect0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int accept0(int fd, int timeoutMs) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.accept0 is implemented in C")
  }
  // The count read, which may be short; 0 at end of stream; negative on failure.
  public static int read0(int fd, byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.read0 is implemented in C")
  }
  // The count written, which is always len or an error: the C loops until the
  // last byte is out, because a short write there is a truncated response.
  public static int write0(int fd, byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.write0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int writeStr0(int fd, String s) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.writeStr0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int close0(int fd) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.close0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int shutdownWrite0(int fd) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.shutdownWrite0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int setTimeout0(int fd, int ms) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.setTimeout0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int setReuse0(int fd, boolean on) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.setReuse0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int localPort0(int fd) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.localPort0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static String localAddr0(int fd) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.localAddr0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static String peerAddr0(int fd) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.peerAddr0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static String strerror0(int code) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.strerror0 is implemented in C")
  }
  // The message for a status code, as the C spells it. The file layer reports
  // its failures with the same codes, so this is what turns one into a sentence
  // for both of them -- and it is the only place a code becomes text.
  public static String strerror(int code) {
    return strerror0(code)
  }
  public static boolean isTimeout0(int code) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.isTimeout0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static boolean isUnknownHost0(int code) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.isUnknownHost0 is implemented in C")
  }
  // The way back from bytes to text. String.getBytes is the way out and is
  // already in the prelude, so only this direction needs anything here. Nothing
  // is decoded: one byte is one character, which is the identity for ASCII and
  // for the UTF-8 that rides on top of it, and it is the same convention
  // String.charAt and String.length already keep.
  public static String stringFrom(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.stringFrom is implemented in C")
  }

  // ---- TLS ----
  //
  // The C half of these is internal/runtime/src/tyrt_tls.c, which the compiler
  // compiles into a build only when the program can reach one of them: that
  // file is the one part of the runtime written against a library the compiler
  // does not ship (OpenSSL), and a program that does not use TLS is not linked
  // against it. Everything below is the same integer convention as above --
  // negative is a code, non-negative is a handle or a count -- with four codes
  // of TLS's own that begin below the socket layer's.
  //
  // A context is a certificate (server) or a trust policy (client), a session
  // is one connection's handshake, and both are small integers the C side keeps
  // in a table. Nothing outside this file calls any of these.

  // A client context. Verification is always on: the empty string means "the
  // system's anchors", and anything else is the PEM text of a CA file added to
  // them. There is no call that turns verification off, and there is not going
  // to be one: see the comment on tyrt_tls.c.
  public static int tlsClientContext0(String caPem) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsClientContext0 is implemented in C")
  }
  // A server context: the certificate and the key as PEM text. The pair is
  // checked when this is called, so a server that cannot present its
  // certificate fails where it is configured rather than at the first client.
  public static int tlsServerContext0(String certPem, String keyPem) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsServerContext0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int tlsFreeContext0(int ctx) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsFreeContext0 is implemented in C")
  }
  // The handshakes. The client's takes the name the certificate has to be for;
  // the server's takes no name, because it is the one presenting a certificate.
  public static int tlsConnect0(int ctx, int fd, String host) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsConnect0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int tlsAccept0(int ctx, int fd) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsAccept0 is implemented in C")
  }
  // The stream, with the contract of read0 and write0: a read returns what the
  // record had, 0 at the end of the stream and a negative code on failure, and
  // a write loops until the last byte is out.
  public static int tlsRead0(int session, byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsRead0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int tlsWrite0(int session, byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsWrite0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int tlsWriteStr0(int session, String s) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsWriteStr0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int tlsClose0(int session) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsClose0 is implemented in C")
  }
  // What the last failure on this thread was about, and which family it belongs
  // to. Both are read straight after the call that failed, on the same thread,
  // which is what makes the answer the right one.
  public static String tlsDetail0() {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsDetail0 is implemented in C")
  }
  public static int tlsKind0(int code) {
    throw new IllegalStateException("Net.tlsKind0 is implemented in C")
  }

  // The exception a code from the runtime means. Returning it rather than
  // throwing it lets a caller write `throw Net.failure(...)` at the one place
  // that knows what was being attempted, and keeps the message there too: the
  // C knows the reason, only the caller knows what the reason is a reason for.
  public static IOException failure(int code, String what) {
    String msg = what + ": " + strerror0(code)
    if (isTimeout0(code)) {
      return new SocketTimeoutException(msg)
    }
    if (isUnknownHost0(code)) {
      return new UnknownHostException(msg)
    }
    return new SocketException(msg)
  }

  // The form used by everything that has an answer on success: a negative code
  // becomes the exception above, anything else passes through.
  public static int ok(int code, String what) {
    if (code < 0) {
      throw failure(code, what)
    }
    return code
  }

  // The same pair for the TLS layer, whose four codes are none of the ones
  // above: a certificate that is not trusted, a certificate that is not for the
  // name, a handshake that failed for another reason, and a certificate or key
  // that cannot be used at all. The reason OpenSSL gave is appended, because
  // the class says which part of the policy was not met and OpenSSL's text says
  // what about it -- "self-signed certificate", "certificate has expired",
  // "Hostname mismatch". A code that is not one of those four is a socket
  // failure or a timeout, and failure() already names those.
  public static IOException tlsFailure(int code, String what) {
    String detail = tlsDetail0()
    if (detail.length() == 0) {
      detail = strerror0(code)
    }
    String msg = what + ": " + detail
    int kind = tlsKind0(code)
    if (kind == 1) {
      return new TlsCertificateException(msg)
    }
    if (kind == 2) {
      return new TlsHostnameException(msg)
    }
    if (kind == 3) {
      return new TlsHandshakeException(msg)
    }
    if (kind == 4) {
      return new TlsConfigException(msg)
    }
    return failure(code, what)
  }

  // A TLS call's answer on success, which is the shape every read and write in
  // this file uses.
  public static int tlsOk(int code, String what) {
    if (code < 0) {
      throw tlsFailure(code, what)
    }
    return code
  }

  public static String stringFrom(byte[] b) {
    return stringFrom(b, 0, b.length)
  }

  // The first len bytes of a String as a byte[], which is getBytes without the
  // rest of the text. A protocol handler that has a header value in hand and an
  // offset into it wants this more often than it wants the whole string.
  public static byte[] bytesOf(String s, int off, int len) {
    byte[] all = s.getBytes()
    byte[] part = new byte[len]
    for (int i = 0 : i < len : i++) {
      part[i] = all[off + i]
    }
    return part
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- ServerSocket

// The listening end. Backlog and the reuse option are constructor arguments, as
// in Java, because both have to be decided before the bind: SO_REUSEADDR set
// afterwards has no effect on a socket that is already bound.
class ServerSocket extends Object implements AutoCloseable {
  private int fd = -1
  private int timeout = 0
  private int port = -1

  public ServerSocket(int port) {
    this(port, 0, true)
  }

  public ServerSocket(int port, int backlog) {
    this(port, backlog, true)
  }

  // reuse defaults to on in every one of these, which Java does not do. Without
  // it, a server that is restarted while a connection is still in TIME_WAIT
  // cannot bind its own port for up to a minute, and the failure looks like
  // "address already in use" on a port nothing is listening on. The one
  // behaviour a test suite and a restarting service both need is the one that
  // should not have to be asked for.
  public ServerSocket(int port, int backlog, boolean reuse) {
    this.port = port
    int r = Net.listen0(port, backlog, reuse)
    if (r < 0) {
      throw Net.failure(r, "bind to port " + port)
    }
    this.fd = r
  }

  // Blocks until a client connects, or until the accept timeout expires, which
  // raises SocketTimeoutException. One connection per call; there is no queue
  // of accepted sockets in this layer, because the kernel already has one and a
  // second copy of it would only add a place for descriptors to leak.
  public Socket accept() {
    requireOpen()
    // Java's meaning of a zero timeout is "no timeout", and this layer keeps
    // that meaning at its own boundary. The C helper uses the opposite
    // convention -- a negative millisecond count waits forever, zero does not
    // wait at all -- so the two have to be translated here. Without this the
    // default ServerSocket, whose timeout starts at zero, never accepts
    // anything: every accept polls with a zero deadline and reports a timeout
    // before the first client can connect.
    int c = Net.accept0(fd, timeout == 0 ? -1 : timeout)
    if (c < 0) {
      throw Net.failure(c, "accept on port " + getLocalPort())
    }
    return new Socket(c)
  }

  // Java's ServerSocket.setSoTimeout bounds accept, and this is the same thing
  // with the same meaning: 0 waits forever. It is what makes a serving loop
  // testable at all -- a program with no threads cannot be interrupted, so
  // without a deadline an accept that nobody connects to hangs the process and
  // there is no way back.
  public void setSoTimeout(int millis) {
    if (millis < 0) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("timeout cannot be negative")
    }
    timeout = millis
  }

  public int getSoTimeout() {
    return timeout
  }

  public void setReuseAddress(boolean on) {
    requireOpen()
    Net.ok(Net.setReuse0(fd, on), "set SO_REUSEADDR")
  }

  // The port the socket is actually bound to, which for `new ServerSocket(0)`
  // is the one the kernel picked. That is how a test binds a port it knows is
  // free without a race against every other process on the machine.
  public int getLocalPort() {
    if (fd < 0) {
      return port
    }
    int p = Net.localPort0(fd)
    if (p < 0) {
      throw Net.failure(p, "read the local port")
    }
    return p
  }

  public boolean isClosed() {
    return fd < 0
  }

  // Closing the listener leaves every accepted connection alone, which is what
  // Java does and what a server that wants to stop listening while it finishes
  // answering wants.
  public void close() {
    if (fd < 0) {
      return
    }
    int f = fd
    fd = -1
    Net.close0(f)
  }

  private void requireOpen() {
    if (fd < 0) {
      throw new SocketException("server socket is closed")
    }
  }

  public String toString() {
    if (fd < 0) {
      return "ServerSocket[closed,port=" + port + "]"
    }
    return "ServerSocket[addr=" + Net.localAddr0(fd) + ",localport=" + getLocalPort() + "]"
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- Socket

// The connected end. Both constructors that take an address connect
// immediately, as java.net.Socket does, so a Socket that exists is a Socket
// that is connected unless it was built from a descriptor.
class Socket extends Object implements AutoCloseable {
  private int fd = -1
  private boolean closed = false
  private int timeout = 0
  private SocketInputStream in = null
  private SocketOutputStream out = null

  // The descriptor form: wraps a socket that is already connected, which is
  // what ServerSocket.accept hands back. Java has the same constructor for the
  // same reason (a SocketImpl), and it is the only way for one class in this
  // package to give another a connected descriptor without a global.
  public Socket(int fd) {
    this.fd = fd
  }

  public Socket(String host, int port) {
    connect(host, port, -1)
  }

  public Socket(String host, int port, int connectTimeoutMs) {
    connect(host, port, connectTimeoutMs)
  }

  public void connect(String host, int port) {
    connect(host, port, -1)
  }

  // A timeout of 0 or less waits without a deadline, which is Java's meaning of
  // "no timeout" and is what `new Socket(host, port)` asks for. Anything else
  // bounds the whole connect, DNS lookup included, and raises
  // SocketTimeoutException when it expires.
  public void connect(String host, int port, int connectTimeoutMs) {
    if (closed) {
      throw new SocketException("socket is closed")
    }
    if (fd >= 0) {
      throw new SocketException("socket is already connected")
    }
    int r = Net.connect0(host, port, connectTimeoutMs > 0 ? connectTimeoutMs : -1)
    if (r < 0) {
      throw Net.failure(r, "connect to " + host + ":" + port)
    }
    fd = r
    // A timeout set before the connection existed still has to take effect, so
    // it is applied now that there is a descriptor to apply it to.
    if (timeout > 0) {
      Net.ok(Net.setTimeout0(fd, timeout), "set SO_RCVTIMEO")
    }
  }

  // The read-side deadline, in Java's units and with Java's meaning: it bounds
  // one read call, not the whole exchange, and 0 waits forever. It is what
  // turns a peer that has gone quiet into a SocketTimeoutException instead of a
  // process that never comes back.
  public void setSoTimeout(int millis) {
    if (millis < 0) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("timeout cannot be negative")
    }
    timeout = millis
    if (fd < 0) {
      return
    }
    Net.ok(Net.setTimeout0(fd, millis), "set SO_RCVTIMEO")
  }

  public int getSoTimeout() {
    return timeout
  }

  public void setReuseAddress(boolean on) {
    requireOpen()
    Net.ok(Net.setReuse0(fd, on), "set SO_REUSEADDR")
  }

  // The end of the stream in one direction only. A client that has sent its
  // whole request and is now waiting for the answer uses this: the FIN tells
  // the server the request is complete, and the socket stays open for the
  // reply, which closing it would throw away.
  public void shutdownOutput() {
    requireOpen()
    Net.ok(Net.shutdownWrite0(fd), "shut down the output side")
  }

  public SocketInputStream getInputStream() {
    requireOpen()
    if (in == null) {
      in = new SocketInputStream(this)
    }
    return in
  }

  public SocketOutputStream getOutputStream() {
    requireOpen()
    if (out == null) {
      out = new SocketOutputStream(this)
    }
    return out
  }

  // Reads for the streams, which have no descriptor of their own and no way to
  // ask this class for one: the field stays private to Socket and the syscalls
  // stay in it too, so there is exactly one owner of the descriptor.
  public int read(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    requireOpen()
    checkBytes(b, off, len)
    if (len == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    return Net.ok(Net.read0(fd, b, off, len), "read")
  }

  public int write(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    requireOpen()
    checkBytes(b, off, len)
    if (len == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    return Net.ok(Net.write0(fd, b, off, len), "write")
  }

  public int write(String s) {
    requireOpen()
    if (s == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("text")
    }
    return Net.ok(Net.writeStr0(fd, s), "write")
  }

  public int getLocalPort() {
    requireOpen()
    return Net.ok(Net.localPort0(fd), "read the local port")
  }

  public String getLocalAddress() {
    requireOpen()
    String a = Net.localAddr0(fd)
    if (a == null) {
      throw new SocketException("read the local address")
    }
    return a
  }

  public String getRemoteAddress() {
    requireOpen()
    String a = Net.peerAddr0(fd)
    if (a == null) {
      throw new SocketException("read the remote address")
    }
    return a
  }

  public boolean isClosed() {
    return closed
  }

  public boolean isConnected() {
    return fd >= 0 && !closed
  }

  // Closing the socket closes both streams, as Java's does: they are views of
  // this descriptor, not owners of it, and a program that writes to a closed
  // socket should get SocketException rather than a descriptor number that has
  // since been handed to a file.
  public void close() {
    if (closed || fd < 0) {
      closed = true
      return
    }
    closed = true
    int f = fd
    fd = -1
    Net.close0(f)
  }

  // The three helpers a subclass needs, and no more than that. TlsSocket is a
  // Socket -- the same object with a TLS session in front of the descriptor --
  // so it reads and writes through the same descriptor with the same checks,
  // and there is still exactly one owner of it: this class. A protected method
  // is what says "this class and its subclass", which is the whole of who may
  // ask these questions.
  protected void requireOpen() {
    if (fd < 0 || closed) {
      throw new SocketException("socket is closed")
    }
  }

  // The argument checks of every read and write. They are one method because
  // the TLS subclass makes the same ones, and two copies of a range check are
  // two places for it to be off by one.
  protected static void checkBytes(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    if (b == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("buffer")
    }
    if (off < 0 || len < 0 || off + len > b.length) {
      throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("off=" + off + " len=" + len + " length=" + b.length)
    }
  }

  // The descriptor this socket is connected on, for the subclass that puts TLS
  // in front of it: the handshake has to be given the connection, and this is
  // the one place that knows the number. Handing it out is not handing it over
  // -- TlsSocket is this object, not a second owner -- and anything that closes
  // this socket closes the TLS connection with it.
  protected int getDescriptor() {
    requireOpen()
    return fd
  }

  // This object has given its descriptor to a TlsSocket built on it, and is
  // finished with it: the descriptor is not closed -- it is in use, by that
  // object -- but this one neither reads, writes nor closes it any more.
  //
  // "Nor closes it" is the whole reason this exists. Two objects holding one
  // descriptor number, both willing to close it, is a close of whatever was
  // handed that number next: the TLS socket's close would close a descriptor
  // that had already been closed and reissued to another connection. Clearing
  // the field makes every one of the three operations answer "closed" instead,
  // which is what this object is.
  protected void handOverDescriptor() {
    fd = -1
    closed = true
  }

  public String toString() {
    if (fd < 0 || closed) {
      return "Socket[closed]"
    }
    return "Socket[addr=" + getRemoteAddress() + ",localport=" + getLocalPort() + "]"
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- streams

// A view of a socket's bytes rather than a stream of its own, which is why it
// holds the Socket and not a descriptor.
//
// It extends the prelude's InputStream so that it can be passed where one is
// expected, and it overrides both of that class's methods: read() because a
// socket is not the console, and readln() because the inherited one reads
// stdin, and a server that silently read the console would be a very quiet bug
// indeed. readLine() is the name this class uses; readln() is kept as the
// override that delegates to it.
class SocketInputStream extends InputStream {
  private Socket socket
  // One buffer for everything: read() and readLine() would otherwise be a
  // syscall per byte, and a request line arriving in two packets is the normal
  // case, not the exceptional one.
  private byte[] buf = new byte[8192]
  private int pos = 0
  private int count = 0

  public SocketInputStream(Socket socket) {
    this.socket = socket
  }

  // Java's contract, byte for byte: the next byte as 0..255, or -1 at the end
  // of the stream. A byte is signed in this language, so the masking is what
  // makes the return value the unsigned one Java promises.
  public int read() {
    if (pos == count) {
      if (!fill()) {
        return -1
      }
    }
    int v = buf[pos]
    pos++
    return v & 255
  }

  // Java's shape: as many bytes as are ready, up to len, and never more. A
  // short result is normal and callers loop; the only two other answers are -1
  // for the end of the stream and an exception for a broken connection or a
  // timeout. When the buffer is empty the read goes straight into the caller's
  // array, so a server that reads request bodies by the kilobyte does not pay
  // for the line buffer it is not using.
  public int read(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    if (b == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("buffer")
    }
    if (off < 0 || len < 0 || off + len > b.length) {
      throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("off=" + off + " len=" + len + " length=" + b.length)
    }
    if (len == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    if (pos < count) {
      int n = count - pos
      if (n > len) {
        n = len
      }
      for (int i = 0 : i < n : i++) {
        b[off + i] = buf[pos + i]
      }
      pos = pos + n
      return n
    }
    // The descriptor answers 0 at the end of the stream -- that is read0's
    // contract, and what fill() checks for -- and this is the layer that speaks
    // Java, where the end of a stream is -1. Passing the 0 on would be a read
    // that made no progress to every caller that loops on `have < len`, and
    // readFully below is one: it would ask again forever rather than throw.
    int n = socket.read(b, off, len)
    return n == 0 ? -1 : n
  }

  // read(byte[],int,int) until the array is full. The distinction this exists
  // for: a short read is a normal answer from read() and a fatal one from
  // readFully, so a caller that needs exactly n bytes -- an HTTP body, a
  // length-prefixed frame -- says so once instead of writing the loop.
  public void readFully(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    int have = 0
    while (have < len) {
      int n = read(b, off + have, len - have)
      if (n < 0) {
        throw new EOFException("end of stream after " + have + " of " + len + " bytes")
      }
      have = have + n
    }
  }

  public byte[] readFully(int len) {
    byte[] b = new byte[len]
    readFully(b, 0, len)
    return b
  }

  // One line, without its terminator, or null at the end of the stream -- the
  // same three-way answer Java's BufferedReader gives: a line, null, or an
  // exception. A terminator is "\n", "\r\n" or a lone "\r", which is what the
  // grammar of every text protocol that predates the web allows.
  //
  // null rather than an exception at the end is deliberate and is why this is
  // not the prelude's readln(): a server loop is `while ((line = in.readLine())
  // != null)`, and making the normal end of a connection an exception would
  // make every loop a try block.
  public String readLine() {
    return readLine(0)
  }

  // readLine(max) is readLine with a limit: a line that has not ended within
  // max bytes stops there and LineTooLongException is thrown rather than the
  // reader holding as much as the peer cares to send. Zero is no limit, which
  // is what a caller reading something it already bounds asks for.
  //
  // The limit counts the bytes of the line and not its characters. A peer that
  // sends a line of four-byte characters is sending four times the bytes, and
  // the number the limit is about is the one the connection carried.
  public String readLine(int max) {
    StringBuilder sb = null
    int got = 0
    while (true) {
      if (pos == count) {
        if (!fill()) {
          if (sb == null) {
            return null
          }
          return sb.toString()
        }
      }
      int nl = -1
      for (int i = pos : i < count : i++) {
        if (buf[i] == 10) {
          nl = i
          break
        }
      }
      if (nl < 0) {
        if (sb == null) {
          sb = new StringBuilder()
        }
        sb.append(Net.stringFrom(buf, pos, count - pos))
        got = got + (count - pos)
        pos = count
        if (max > 0 && got > max) {
          throw new LineTooLongException("a line longer than " + max + " bytes")
        }
        continue
      }
      got = got + (nl - pos)
      if (max > 0 && got > max) {
        throw new LineTooLongException("a line longer than " + max + " bytes")
      }
      String line
      if (sb == null) {
        line = Net.stringFrom(buf, pos, nl - pos)
      } else {
        sb.append(Net.stringFrom(buf, pos, nl - pos))
        line = sb.toString()
      }
      pos = nl + 1
      if (line.endsWith("\r")) {
        line = line.substring(0, line.length() - 1)
      }
      return line
    }
  }

  // The prelude's InputStream has readln() and this is a socket, not the
  // console. Overriding it is not a nicety: without it, `in.readln()` on a
  // socket would compile, run, and read the terminal.
  public String readln() {
    return readLine()
  }

  public boolean isClosed() {
    return socket.isClosed()
  }

  // Closing a stream closes its socket, as in Java. The stream is a view, so
  // there is nothing here to close that the socket does not own.
  public void close() {
    socket.close()
  }

  // Nothing to do: the C writes every call straight to the socket, so there is
  // no user-space buffer to push out. Java's socket streams have the same
  // no-op flush() and for the same reason.
  public void flush() {
  }

  // Fills the buffer, returning false at the end of the stream. A short read is
  // not the end: read() returns what the kernel had, and the next call asks
  // again. Only a 0 means the peer is done.
  private boolean fill() {
    int n = socket.read(buf, 0, buf.length)
    if (n <= 0) {
      return false
    }
    pos = 0
    count = n
    return true
  }
}

// The writing side. Every write reaches the socket before it returns: the C
// loops until the last byte is out, so a short write is not something a caller
// here has to consider. flush() exists for the shape of the API and does
// nothing, which is what a socket stream's flush does in Java too.
class SocketOutputStream extends Object implements AutoCloseable {
  private Socket socket
  private byte[] one = new byte[1]

  public SocketOutputStream(Socket socket) {
    this.socket = socket
  }

  // Java's OutputStream.write(int) writes the low eight bits and nothing else,
  // so a byte that arrives as 0..255 or as -128..127 means the same thing here.
  public void write(int b) {
    one[0] = (byte) b
    socket.write(one, 0, 1)
  }

  public void write(byte[] b) {
    write(b, 0, b.length)
  }

  public void write(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    socket.write(b, off, len)
  }

  // Not in java.io, where the same thing is an OutputStreamWriter around this
  // one. A protocol's bytes are text far more often than not, and a socket that
  // can write a String without a conversion in the caller is the difference
  // between one line and three at every call site.
  public void write(String s) {
    socket.write(s)
  }

  public boolean isClosed() {
    return socket.isClosed()
  }

  public void close() {
    socket.close()
  }

  public void flush() {
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- TLS

// The policy half of TLS: where the certificates come from. The stream half is
// TlsSocket below, and the two are apart because they have different lifetimes
// -- a context is built once and used for every connection, a session exists
// for one connection -- and because a caller that has a certificate in hand
// (from a keystore, an environment variable, a test's own constant) wants to
// hand it over as text, while a caller that has a path wants to give a path.
//
// The names are Spring's, because the shape of the problem is the same one:
// `server.ssl.certificate` and `server.ssl.certificate-private-key` are a path
// to a certificate chain and a path to its private key, and `ssl(cert, key)` on
// the server is those two properties.
final class Tls {
  // The client context a program that names no CA file uses, built when it is
  // first asked for and kept for the life of the process. Building one loads
  // the system's trust anchors; a client that built one per request would load
  // them per request, and every request in this prelude builds a client.
  private static int defaultClient = -1

  // A client context. An empty caFile is the system's anchors and nothing else;
  // anything else is a PEM file whose certificates are added to them, which is
  // how a server with its own CA -- or a test's self-signed certificate -- is
  // trusted without giving up on the anchors every other name is checked
  // against. There is no way to ask for no verification at all.
  public static int clientContext(String caFile) {
    if (caFile.length() == 0) {
      if (defaultClient < 0) {
        defaultClient = Net.tlsOk(Net.tlsClientContext0(""), "create the TLS client context")
      }
      return defaultClient
    }
    return Net.tlsOk(Net.tlsClientContext0(pemOf(caFile)), "load " + caFile)
  }

  // A server context: the certificate chain and the key, both PEM files. Both
  // are read here and checked against each other by the C half, so a server
  // whose key does not belong to its certificate hears about it now.
  public static int serverContext(String certificate, String privateKey) {
    return Net.tlsOk(Net.tlsServerContext0(pemOf(certificate), pemOf(privateKey)),
        "load " + certificate + " and " + privateKey)
  }

  // The PEM text of a file, which is what both the C half and the exceptions
  // above are written in terms of. The bytes are not decoded or validated: a
  // PEM document is ASCII, and a file that is not one is refused by the parser
  // that reads it with the reason, which is a better message than a decoder
  // could give.
  public static String pemOf(String path) {
    return Files.readString(path)
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- TlsSocket

// A socket whose bytes go through TLS.
//
// It is a Socket rather than something that has one, and that is what makes the
// rest of this file work unchanged: SocketInputStream and SocketOutputStream
// hold a Socket and read and write through it, the server's loop reads a
// request line off the same stream whether the connection is TLS or not, and a
// WebSocket runs over a TLS connection without knowing it. What changes is the
// four methods below, and nothing above them.
//
// A connection is made plaintext and then upgraded, which is how every TLS
// library does it and what makes one connect path serve both: `new
// Socket(host, port)`, setSoTimeout, then start(...). The descriptor changes
// hands in that call and not before: until the handshake has worked the
// plaintext socket is the one that owns it, and from then on this one is --
// the socket it was built from is told so (handOverDescriptor) and answers
// "closed" to everything, so there is one owner at every moment and never a
// second close of a descriptor number that has since been reused. Everything
// after the handshake is this class's four methods; everything above them is
// Socket's.
class TlsSocket extends Socket {
  // The session, or -1 before the handshake has happened. Only the factories
  // below set it, and each of them returns only after a handshake that worked.
  private int session = -1

  private TlsSocket(int fd) {
    super(fd)
  }

  // The client handshake, with the system's trust anchors. The host is what the
  // certificate has to be for: it is checked against the name in the
  // certificate, and a certificate for another name is refused rather than
  // accepted, which is the difference between TLS and a private conversation
  // with whoever answered.
  public static TlsSocket start(Socket plain, String host) {
    return start(plain, host, "")
  }

  // The same, with a PEM file whose certificates are trusted as well.
  public static TlsSocket start(Socket plain, String host, String caFile) {
    if (plain == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("socket")
    }
    TlsSocket secure = new TlsSocket(plain.getDescriptor())
    try {
      secure.handshake(Tls.clientContext(caFile), host, false)
    } catch (IOException e) {
      // The connection is over, and the descriptor is the plaintext socket's to
      // close: closing it from here as well would be the same descriptor closed
      // twice, which on a busy process is somebody else's socket. The caller's
      // own close() of the socket it passed in is then a no-op, because that
      // object is the one that did it.
      plain.close()
      throw e
    }
    // From here the descriptor is this TlsSocket's: the plaintext object it was
    // read from is finished with it, and says so, so that a later close of that
    // object cannot close a number that has since been handed to another
    // connection.
    plain.handOverDescriptor()
    return secure
  }

  // The server handshake, on a connection a listener just accepted, with the
  // certificate and key the context was made from. There is no host to check:
  // the certificate is ours and it is the client's business whether it accepts
  // it.
  public static TlsSocket accept(Socket plain, int ctx) {
    if (plain == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("socket")
    }
    TlsSocket secure = new TlsSocket(plain.getDescriptor())
    try {
      secure.handshake(ctx, "", true)
    } catch (IOException e) {
      plain.close()
      throw e
    }
    plain.handOverDescriptor()
    return secure
  }

  private void handshake(int ctx, String host, boolean server) {
    int h
    if (server) {
      h = Net.tlsAccept0(ctx, getDescriptor())
    } else {
      h = Net.tlsConnect0(ctx, getDescriptor(), host)
    }
    if (h < 0) {
      // A handshake that failed has no session behind it, so there is nothing
      // here to free: the factories above close the connection, and the message
      // names the host rather than this socket's port, because the caller
      // already knows which connection it was and a port number in a message is
      // one more thing that differs between two runs of the same program.
      throw Net.tlsFailure(h, server ? "TLS handshake with " + getRemoteAddress() : "TLS handshake with " + host)
    }
    session = h
  }

  // Whether this connection is encrypted, which is true from the moment a
  // factory returned it.
  public boolean isSecure() {
    return session >= 0
  }

  public int read(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    requireOpen()
    checkBytes(b, off, len)
    if (len == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    return Net.tlsOk(Net.tlsRead0(session, b, off, len), "read over TLS")
  }

  public int write(byte[] b, int off, int len) {
    requireOpen()
    checkBytes(b, off, len)
    if (len == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    return Net.tlsOk(Net.tlsWrite0(session, b, off, len), "write over TLS")
  }

  public int write(String s) {
    requireOpen()
    if (s == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("text")
    }
    return Net.tlsOk(Net.tlsWriteStr0(session, s), "write over TLS")
  }

  // The half close has no TLS equivalent and this says so rather than pretending
  // otherwise. What ends a TLS stream is the close_notify of close(): TLS has no
  // message for "my half is finished and yours is not", and a TCP FIN sent
  // under a TLS session is a truncated stream that the peer reads as a
  // connection that went away mid-record -- which is exactly the failure a
  // caller trying to be tidy would be creating.
  public void shutdownOutput() {
    throw new UnsupportedOperationException(
        "shutdownOutput is not available on a TLS socket: close() sends the close_notify that ends the stream")
  }

  // Ends the TLS session and then the descriptor. The close_notify goes out
  // before the socket is closed, so the peer reads the end of the stream as an
  // end rather than as a connection that broke.
  public void close() {
    if (session >= 0) {
      Net.tlsClose0(session)
      session = -1
    }
    super.close()
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- TlsServer

// The certificate and the key a server presents, as the one object that owns
// them: a context, and the connections accepted into it.
//
// It exists so that an HttpServer can be given a certificate without knowing
// anything about TLS: the server holds one of these or holds nothing, and
// whether a connection is wrapped is a question about an object rather than a
// branch in the serving loop. It also means the certificate is read and checked
// once, when the server is configured, rather than on every connection.
class TlsServer {
  private final int ctx

  public TlsServer(String certificate, String privateKey) {
    this.ctx = Tls.serverContext(certificate, privateKey)
  }

  // The handshake on a connection a listener just accepted.
  public Socket accept(Socket plain) {
    return TlsSocket.accept(plain, ctx)
  }

  // A context is freed when the server that owns it is closed. A caller that
  // builds one without a server has to free it the same way; there is no
  // finalizer here, and a context that is never freed is a copy of the
  // certificate that lives until the process ends. A second close frees
  // nothing -- the layer answers "no such context" and there is no context
  // left to answer for -- which is what makes closing one safe to do from a
  // finally block.
  public void close() {
    Net.tlsFreeContext0(ctx)
  }
}
